course: dictionary
lesson: 3009
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A word can have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one is meant. The word dutch is a good example. It can describe people or things from the Netherlands, it can name a language, and it is part of the phrase «go Dutch».
explain: Look at the words around it. In «Dutch cheese», Dutch comes before a noun, so it describes the cheese. In «She speaks Dutch», it names a language. Ask yourself which meaning fits the sentence, then try it in place of the word to check.
explain: Some words have a history too. «In the 1500s "bully" meant darling or sweetheart, probably from a Dutch word.» Its meaning changed over time, so knowing where a word came from can help you understand what it means today.
fact: Dutch can be an adjective: it describes something from the Netherlands, which is also called Holland.
fact: Dutch can be a noun: it is the language spoken in the Netherlands and part of Belgium.
fact: «Go Dutch» means each person pays for their own share of a meal or outing.
fact: Several English words came from Dutch, such as «cookie», «boss» and «yacht».
fact: The Dutch reach means opening a car door with the hand farther from it, so you turn your body and look back for bikes.
exercise: «We will go Dutch, so each of us pays for our own pizza.» What does go Dutch mean here? | *Each person pays for their own share | We eat with a fork and knife | We pay with Dutch coins | One person pays for everyone
exercise: «The Dutch live in a flat country with windmills and canals.» Who are the Dutch in this sentence? | A game with two ropes | A type of cheese | *The people from the Netherlands | A language spoken in Belgium
exercise: Find the English word that came from Dutch. | hill | *cookie | castle | stood
test: In «Dutch cheese», what job does Dutch do? | It names the language | *It describes the cheese | It names a person | It means a meal
test: Which phrase means each person pays their own share? | Dutch cheese | *Go Dutch | Dutch reach | Double Dutch
exam: In the sentence about Malacca, «the Dutch took it in 1641», who took the port? | *The Dutch | Portugal | Malacca | The Banda Islands
